WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:03.440
- Hi, I'm Rie, I'm a Tasty producer in LA.

00:00:03.440 --> 00:00:06.040
Today, I'm going to be showing you

00:00:06.040 --> 00:00:10.020
how to make most mesmerizing
Japanese dessert.

00:00:10.020 --> 00:00:14.020
Japanese people, including
me, are obsessed with seasons.

00:00:14.020 --> 00:00:17.070
Cherry blossom season is
such a short amount of time,

00:00:17.070 --> 00:00:20.880
so from the end of March
to beginning of April,

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:23.760
a lot of people go for picnics, hiking,

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:26.810
and have a party under
the cherry blossoms.

00:00:26.810 --> 00:00:30.340
It's my favorite season
and I miss Japan so much,

00:00:30.340 --> 00:00:32.660
especially around this time of the year,

00:00:32.660 --> 00:00:34.940
because LA has no seasons.

00:00:34.940 --> 00:00:38.120
I think our ancestors started to preserve

00:00:38.120 --> 00:00:43.000
this beautiful flowers so
they could enjoy it longer.

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:45.120
Traditionally, raindrop cake,

00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:48.180
in Japanese we call mizu shingen mochi,

00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:50.137
is made with agar-agar.

00:00:50.137 --> 00:00:54.010
Agar-agar is a jelly-like
substance made from algae.

00:00:54.010 --> 00:00:57.339
So unlike gelatin, it's vegetarian.

00:00:57.339 --> 00:00:59.510
Agar itself has no flavor,

00:00:59.510 --> 00:01:03.300
so raindrop cakes taste
like sugar water Jell-O,

00:01:03.300 --> 00:01:06.100
so pour your favorite syrup on top.

00:01:06.100 --> 00:01:07.710
Japanese people also value

00:01:07.710 --> 00:01:09.750
beautiful looking desserts and food.

00:01:09.750 --> 00:01:11.250
No offense to your brownies

00:01:11.250 --> 00:01:13.900
or chocolate chip cookies,
those are delicious,

00:01:13.900 --> 00:01:17.560
but maybe not quite as visually pleasing.

00:01:17.560 --> 00:01:19.810
You are also eating with your eyes,

00:01:19.810 --> 00:01:23.450
and this raindrop cake
is a perfect example.

00:01:23.450 --> 00:01:27.160
The cherry blossoms are
encased in a drop of water.

00:01:27.160 --> 00:01:30.520
It reminds me of cherry
blossom on a rainy day.

00:01:30.520 --> 00:01:33.270
(tranquil music)

00:01:35.030 --> 00:01:39.000
I think mochi is getting
popular all over the world.

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:41.070
The powder I used for this video,

00:01:41.070 --> 00:01:44.790
called shiratamako, it
is made with rice flour.

00:01:44.790 --> 00:01:48.030
I used matcha for the ice cream flavor.

00:01:48.030 --> 00:01:49.850
Matcha is powdered green tea.

00:01:49.850 --> 00:01:53.890
It's packed with antioxidants
and boosts your metabolism

00:01:53.890 --> 00:01:56.950
so you can eat matcha ice
cream without feeling guilty.

00:01:56.950 --> 00:02:00.700
Mochi is super easy to
make and easy to work with,

00:02:00.700 --> 00:02:03.960
but it is very sticky,
so make sure you use

00:02:03.960 --> 00:02:06.210
a lot of starch on the surface.

00:02:06.210 --> 00:02:09.450
Typically, mochi's made
with steamed mochi rice

00:02:09.450 --> 00:02:12.130
and eaten mainly around New Year's.

00:02:12.130 --> 00:02:15.550
However, the mochi we
used for mochi ice cream,

00:02:15.550 --> 00:02:18.360
we call gyuhi, used all year round.

00:02:18.360 --> 00:02:21.350
I like matcha flavor because
it's a little bitter,

00:02:21.350 --> 00:02:24.210
so it's a good balance to the ice cream.

00:02:24.210 --> 00:02:26.470
I made this video for Tasty Japan.

00:02:26.470 --> 00:02:28.810
I wanted to make a mochi ice cream video,

00:02:28.810 --> 00:02:31.780
but mochi ice cream is
cheap, so I was wondering

00:02:31.780 --> 00:02:35.570
how I could convince our
audience to make mochi ice cream.

00:02:35.570 --> 00:02:37.650
And I was like, okay, let's make it giant,

00:02:37.650 --> 00:02:40.620
'cause I've never seen giant
mochi ice cream before.

00:02:40.620 --> 00:02:41.540
If you don't have matcha,

00:02:41.540 --> 00:02:45.160
you can use cocoa powder
or strawberry jam.

00:02:45.160 --> 00:02:46.680
After we published the video,

00:02:46.680 --> 00:02:49.710
I saw that some of our
audience actually made it

00:02:49.710 --> 00:02:51.960
and tagged us on our Instagram account.

00:02:51.960 --> 00:02:53.700
It made me very happy.

00:02:53.700 --> 00:02:55.470
It's one of the best feeling I get

00:02:55.470 --> 00:02:58.050
from being a Tasty video producer.

00:02:58.050 --> 00:02:59.390
As I mentioned earlier,

00:02:59.390 --> 00:03:02.600
Japanese people obsessed
with seasonal ingredients.

00:03:02.600 --> 00:03:04.450
Pumpkin and chestnut flavors

00:03:04.450 --> 00:03:07.170
are very popular during the fall season.

00:03:07.170 --> 00:03:09.663
We used kabocha pumpkins for our video.

00:03:09.663 --> 00:03:12.820
Kabocha is the most
popular pumpkin in Japan.

00:03:12.820 --> 00:03:16.720
They are sweet and a dryer
texture than butternut squash,

00:03:16.720 --> 00:03:20.910
perfect for baking and
cooking and I love using them.

00:03:20.910 --> 00:03:22.830
Kabocha is getting popular in America.

00:03:22.830 --> 00:03:25.400
I can find them in grocery stores easily.

00:03:25.400 --> 00:03:28.680
Japanese people have started
celebrating Halloween,

00:03:28.680 --> 00:03:32.000
but Halloween in Japan is
more popular among adults.

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:35.220
Dressing up in costume is
not for trick-or-treating.

00:03:35.220 --> 00:03:37.370
Some of the Western traditional holidays

00:03:37.370 --> 00:03:39.610
are misinterpreted in Japan.

00:03:39.610 --> 00:03:42.020
We celebrate Christmas, but it is more

00:03:42.020 --> 00:03:44.470
like a romantic holiday for couples.

00:03:44.470 --> 00:03:47.130
So this kabocha pumpkin pudding was made

00:03:47.130 --> 00:03:49.270
by Tasty Japan producer Saki,

00:03:49.270 --> 00:03:52.600
but she was struggling
with unmolding the pudding.

00:03:52.600 --> 00:03:55.890
So I learned this cool
trick on the internet.

00:03:55.890 --> 00:03:59.170
This is how to unmold
pudding in a bundt pan.

00:03:59.170 --> 00:04:03.063
So hold tight your bundt pan
and plate, and you just spin.

00:04:08.387 --> 00:04:13.220
'Kay, made me a little bit
dizzy but, pudding is unmolded.

00:04:15.080 --> 00:04:16.740
So this pumpkin pudding,

00:04:16.740 --> 00:04:19.010
it's not your typical Halloween treat,

00:04:19.010 --> 00:04:21.010
but I think it's really pretty

00:04:21.010 --> 00:04:24.790
because of the color contrast,
yellow and shiny brown.

00:04:24.790 --> 00:04:26.760
And also, when you pour the chocolate,

00:04:26.760 --> 00:04:29.240
I feel like it look like a spider web.

00:04:29.240 --> 00:04:31.793
So, I think it's a
perfect Halloween dessert.

00:04:33.120 --> 00:04:34.850
Some of you watching this video

00:04:34.850 --> 00:04:38.310
might have seen this recipe
already, but stay with me.

00:04:38.310 --> 00:04:42.820
It's one of the most viewed
and shared Tasty Japan video.

00:04:42.820 --> 00:04:46.150
I didn't know how deeply
orange the yolk of the egg

00:04:46.150 --> 00:04:48.700
until I moved from Japan to the states.

00:04:48.700 --> 00:04:52.830
As you can see Tasty producer
Alvin made the video in Japan,

00:04:52.830 --> 00:04:55.810
so the egg yolk is very orange.

00:04:55.810 --> 00:04:57.810
Alvin and I went to Japan together

00:04:57.810 --> 00:05:00.490
a year ago for research purposes.

00:05:00.490 --> 00:05:03.390
Alvin was obsessed with
this jiggly cheesecake

00:05:03.390 --> 00:05:05.960
that he saw on the internet.

00:05:05.960 --> 00:05:09.120
We went to the store and
watch how they make them.

00:05:09.120 --> 00:05:11.810
So this cheesecake you can eat year round,

00:05:11.810 --> 00:05:14.210
but I chose it for winter dessert

00:05:14.210 --> 00:05:17.120
because you want to enjoy while it's hot,

00:05:17.120 --> 00:05:20.660
you wanna eat it right after
came out from the oven.

00:05:20.660 --> 00:05:23.683
So I think this is a
perfect dessert for winter.

00:05:25.030 --> 00:05:27.620
I've started cooking more
and more since I moved

00:05:27.620 --> 00:05:31.930
to the United States, mainly
as a homesickness cure.

00:05:31.930 --> 00:05:34.260
Even though I'm not physically home,

00:05:34.260 --> 00:05:37.520
it feels like home wherever I am

00:05:37.520 --> 00:05:39.860
when I cook and eat Japanese food.

00:05:39.860 --> 00:05:43.130
I didn't have a lot of friends
when I moved to the states,

00:05:43.130 --> 00:05:45.550
but whenever you meet new people,

00:05:45.550 --> 00:05:49.250
I've found that talking about
food is a great icebreaker.

00:05:49.250 --> 00:05:52.200
Regardless of their age, gender, or race,

00:05:52.200 --> 00:05:55.780
everyone has their own
personal stories about food.

00:05:55.780 --> 00:05:58.980
I love everything about
Japanese dessert and culture,

00:05:58.980 --> 00:06:01.053
I'd love to hear your stories.

00:06:03.288 --> 00:06:05.057
- [Voiceover] Oh, yes!

00:06:05.057 --> 00:06:07.640
(serene music)

00:06:16.742 --> 00:06:20.242
(camera shutter clicking)

